<title>Pre-Assignment: Pancake's Pace Tracker -- Running the Rules in Reverse</title>

<p>
  Pancake the alpaca has a new hobby: training for the Great Pasture Race.
  The farmer (that's you) strapped a fitness tracker to her harness, and now
  you've got speed data streaming in. Before we meet Thursday, let's see
  what you can figure out just by thinking backwards -- it's exactly the
  kind of reverse-engineering we'll be doing together in class.
</p>

<p>
  <alert>Question 1.</alert> Pancake starts her warm-up lap running at a
  steady, constant speed of <m>5</m> meters per second. If she keeps that
  pace for <m>12</m> seconds, how many meters does she cover?
</p>

<answer>
  <label>Distance at constant speed</label>
  <award>60</award>
</answer>

<p>
  Easy enough: speed times time. But Pancake doesn't stay at a constant
  pace for long -- she's a racer, so she accelerates. Once her speed is a
  changing function of time instead of a fixed number, you can't just
  multiply anymore. Here's the puzzle hiding underneath: is there some
  <em>position</em> function whose rate of change matches Pancake's speed
  function exactly?
</p>

<p>
  <alert>Question 2.</alert> Suppose Pancake's speed at time <m>t</m>
  seconds into her sprint is <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m> meters per second. Coach
  wants a position function <m>s(t)</m> whose derivative is exactly
  <m>v(t)</m>. Check each option below using the differentiation rules you
  already know -- take the derivative of each one and see which actually
  matches <m>3t^2</m>.
</p>

<answer>
  <label>Which expression has derivative v(t) = 3t^2?</label>
  <choice credit="1"><m>t^3</m></choice>
  <choice credit="0"><m>6t</m></choice>
  <choice credit="0"><m>t^3/3</m></choice>
  <choice credit="0"><m>3t^3</m></choice>
</answer>

<p>
  Here's a fun wrinkle: <m>t^3 + 100</m> also has derivative <m>3t^2</m>.
  So does <m>t^3 - 7</m>. Once you find one correct position function, you
  can shift it up or down by any constant and the rate of change never
  notices. Hang onto that observation -- it's going to matter a lot on
  Thursday.
</p>

<p>
  <alert>Question 3.</alert> Here's the general pattern: you already know
  that the derivative of <m>x^n</m> is <m>n x^{n-1}</m> (that's the power
  rule from the differentiation toolkit). Reversing that rule tells you
  exactly how to build a function whose derivative is any power of
  <m>x</m>. Use the reverse pattern now: what is a function whose
  derivative is <m>4x^3</m>?
</p>

<answer>
  <label>A function whose derivative is 4x^3</label>
  <award>x^4</award>
</answer>

<p>
  Hold onto that reverse trick -- on Thursday we'll give it a name
  (antiderivative), a symbol, and a full toolkit for running every
  derivative rule you know backwards.
</p>
